      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE REHNQUIST: We'll hear argument now in No. 02-6320, John J. Fellers v. the United States."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner (Waxman)",
      "case_id": "2003-02-6320",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ad9797ec722593b68c1ac8140c40d9cdec4bff17e406a7bb3a7151b6990aece9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ad9797ec7225-0000-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "the constitutional right involved is the Sixth Amendment right, unlike in Miranda, the Fifth Amendment right of self-incrimination."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kennedy",
      "case_id": "2003-02-6320",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ad9797ec722593b68c1ac8140c40d9cdec4bff17e406a7bb3a7151b6990aece9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ad9797ec7225-0000-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 5,
        "start": 5
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: Do police officers generally know this distinction, that when an indictment has been handed down, suddenly the Sixth Amendment is in the case as well as the Fifth?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2003-02-6320",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ad9797ec722593b68c1ac8140c40d9cdec4bff17e406a7bb3a7151b6990aece9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ad9797ec7225-0000-0005",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 5,
        "start": 5
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "nonetheless the fruits of that confession have to be suppressed under the derivative evidence rule, unless the Government carries its burden to prove sufficient attenuation of taint."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner (Waxman)",
      "case_id": "2003-02-6320",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ad9797ec722593b68c1ac8140c40d9cdec4bff17e406a7bb3a7151b6990aece9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ad9797ec7225-0000-0006",
      "kind": "stated_consequence",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 5,
        "start": 5
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "the fruits of that statement under Nix and Wade have to be suppressed."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2003-02-6320",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ad9797ec722593b68c1ac8140c40d9cdec4bff17e406a7bb3a7151b6990aece9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ad9797ec7225-0000-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 10,
        "start": 10
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: -- or a legal conclusion? I mean, it seems to me he can -- he can find as a fact what the officer said, but whether it constitutes deliberate elicitation within the meaning of our - of our opinion, it seems to me, is a legal question."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Respondent",
      "case_id": "2003-02-6320",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ad9797ec722593b68c1ac8140c40d9cdec4bff17e406a7bb3a7151b6990aece9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ad9797ec7225-0001-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. WAXMAN: Yes, Your Honor. Now, the -- the Eighth Circuit was incorrect, because it applied the wrong standard. It asked whether there was interrogation, when this Court made clear in Rhode Island v. Innis that that is not the test under the Sixth Amendment for good reasons, and in any event, this was the, quote, functional equivalent of interrogation."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2003-02-6320",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ad9797ec722593b68c1ac8140c40d9cdec4bff17e406a7bb3a7151b6990aece9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ad9797ec7225-0001-0003",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 30,
        "start": 30
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "On the central legal issue in this case, the critical fact is that, at the jailhouse, after petitioner was transported from his home, petitioner received a full set of Miranda warnings, which apprised him of his right to counsel, and knowingly, voluntarily, and intelligently waived his right to counsel."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Government",
      "case_id": "2003-02-6320",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "ad9797ec722593b68c1ac8140c40d9cdec4bff17e406a7bb3a7151b6990aece9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ad9797ec7225-0002-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "the deliberate elicitation standard, as so phrased, gives rise to some confusion, because it does suggest that there's a subjective component to it, where deliberate elicitation does have a different application than interrogation for purposes of Miranda with respect to undercover agents."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court Transcript",
      "case_id": "2003-02-6320",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "ad9797ec722593b68c1ac8140c40d9cdec4bff17e406a7bb3a7151b6990aece9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ad9797ec7225-0002-0001",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 39,
        "start": 39
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "What happened is, the officers arrived at petitioner's home. The officers knew petitioner. This was not somebody that they had never met before."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court Transcript",
      "case_id": "2003-02-6320",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "ad9797ec722593b68c1ac8140c40d9cdec4bff17e406a7bb3a7151b6990aece9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ad9797ec7225-0002-0003",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 42,
        "start": 42
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "the officers interrupted him, cut him off, and said it's time to go, John, you know. And John said, can I please get some shoes on? And they accompanied him downstairs, he got shoes, then they took him down to the jailhouse."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Rehnquist",
      "case_id": "2003-02-6320",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "ad9797ec722593b68c1ac8140c40d9cdec4bff17e406a7bb3a7151b6990aece9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ad9797ec7225-0003-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 56,
        "start": 56
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE REHNQUIST: Thank you, Mr.\nDreeben. The case is submitted."
    }
  ],
